IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: W.P. (C) No. 217 of 2019
CITATION: (2020) 10 SCC 274
YEAR: 2020

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: R.F. Nariman, Aniruddha Bose, V. Ramasubramanian

PARTIES:
IAMAI vs Reserve Bank of India

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Constitution of India, Banking Regulation Act, 1949

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Article 19(1)(g), Article 14

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
virtual currencies, cryptocurrency circular, banking prohibition, proportionality test

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Set aside the RBI circular banning banks from providing services to virtual currency exchanges, ruling that the blanket ban failed the constitutional proportionality test under Article 19(1)(g).

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Article 19(1)(g), Article 14.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

